   "id": "79cedbdc-2a4d-465a-ada5-12f23bf033a3",
   "term": "PPM (peak program meter)",
   "verdict": "MINOR",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "scenario_contexts",
     "severity": "Medium",
     "issue": "web-verified via Wikipedia 'Peak programme meter' / IEC 60268-10: a standard quasi-peak PPM (Type I integration time 5 ms, Type II 10 ms) deliberately under-reads true peaks by ~6-8 dB on real signals and its short integration time was chosen specifically to ignore the fastest transients. The scenario 'Setting broadcast levels off a PPM so a sharp transient peak doesn't sneak past and over-modulate' is backwards: brief transient peaks DO sneak past a quasi-peak PPM (this is exactly why sample/true-peak meters exist).",
     "suggestion": "Reword to reflect quasi-peak behaviour, e.g. 'Setting broadcast levels off a PPM so sustained programme peaks are controlled - while remembering a quasi-peak PPM under-reads very short transients, which is why a true-peak (dBTP) meter is used to guard against inter-sample/over-modulation on sharp peaks.'"
    },
    {
     "field": "plain_english",
     "severity": "Low",
     "issue": "web-verified via IEC 60268-10 (integration time 5-10 ms): a standard quasi-peak PPM does not show the true maximum of a signal; it under-reads short peaks by ~6-8 dB. The phrase 'So you can read the true maximums' overstates this and blurs the PPM vs true-peak-meter distinction the entry otherwise draws.",
     "suggestion": "Change 'So you can read the true maximums' to something like 'So you can read sustained peaks far more reliably than a VU meter (though a quasi-peak PPM still slightly under-reads the very fastest transients).'"
    }
   ]
  },
